WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:18.960
You’ve probably seen this ad.
Dollar Shave Club might’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.760
the biggest Direct to Consumer brand.
So, let’s look at their ads today.&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.840
This brand was everywhere. It reshaped the&nbsp;
overpriced razor market. It bent Gillette&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.040
into lowering prices.
Customers loved it.&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.520
So, how did it go from this to this?
Unfortunately, Dollar Shave Club&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.360
was broken from the start.
This is the rise and strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.720
fall of Dollar Shave Club.
CHAPTER 1 - The $4500 Ad.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.760
Dollar Shave Club, which I’ll call “DSC”&nbsp;
from here on out, was one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.480
iconic “direct to consumer brands”.
You bought a box with a razor, some blades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.000
and their famous shave butter, and received&nbsp;
monthly razors and products in their subscription.&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.160
But, if you go to DollarShaveClub&nbsp;
today, you’ll find no subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.000
only items to purchase. On their Australian&nbsp;
website, you’ll see “No Subscription Required.”&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
That’s a clue to what happened, and&nbsp;
the problem they had, from the start.&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.080
But to understand that, we&nbsp;
need to look at why DSC worked.&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.720
The razor market was immensely profitable.&nbsp;
Really really profitable, because companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.280
like Gillette, came up with a simple trick.
Sell the handle for cheap, but design it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.560
so only your blades fit. Then make your real&nbsp;
money selling replacement cartridges for years.&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.960
They sold the handles cheap, then charged&nbsp;
$2 to $6 per replacement cartridge.&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.680
which are dirt cheap to produce.
Meaning margins are above 60%.&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:22.480
It was so profitable that P&amp;G paid $57&nbsp;
billion to buy the company in 2005.&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.840
Of course, the real cost was that breaking&nbsp;
into manufacturing required a massive upfront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.840
investment, and convincing giant&nbsp;
retailers to stock your products.&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.520
Gillette and brands like Schick dominated the&nbsp;
retail game, and new players couldn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.880
get in. These two brands controlled&nbsp;
90% of the men’s shaving market.&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.240
With all that said, you can see why customers&nbsp;
loved the move by Michael Dubin and Mark Levine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.640
who were trying to figure out how to move&nbsp;
a giant warehouse of razors they imported.&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.080
That’s right, DSC didn’t actually make any blades.&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.240
They imported rebadged blades from&nbsp;
South Korean manufacturer Dorco.&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:06.000
Their "Executive" 6-blade cartridge was&nbsp;
the Dorco Pace 6, sold under DSC branding.&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.560
But, this was really smart, because&nbsp;
Dorco blades were excellent.&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.480
But how do you break into the market?
Then came the idea: Dollar Shave Club.&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.240
Mail razor blades to customers every month, for&nbsp;
just a few dollars. There was no retail markup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.360
nor the gigantic Gillette profit margins.
Customers would pay less, save time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.560
have more convenience, and everyone&nbsp;
wins. At least, so they thought.&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.480
This alone doesn't explain the&nbsp;
wild, instant success they had.&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.520
For that, they needed some good marketing,&nbsp;
which, luckily, was Dubin’s background.&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.680
This is, in my opinion, one&nbsp;
of the best ads ever produced.&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.600
It’s funny, it pointed out a common&nbsp;
problem, proposed the solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:16.640
explained the business model, and really&nbsp;
catered to the “no nonsense” trend of the 2010s.&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.680
I think MBAs will be studying this ad for years.
Dubin wrote the script himself and then had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:28.480
his friend shoot it all in one day for $4500.
This went live at 6am, and within an hour and 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.920
minutes, the site was so flooded with orders that&nbsp;
it crashed. By the time it was fixed the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.200
they received over 12,000 order notifications.
Their original supply disappeared almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.120
immediately. So Dubin rushed to hire contractors&nbsp;
and import more blades to keep customers supplied.&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.400
This ad won many awards, including the&nbsp;
best consumer video campaign from Digiday.&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.120
People loved Dollar Shave&nbsp;
Club, and it was growing. Fast.&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:01.360
“Online manual blade and razor sales&nbsp;
jumped 97% in 2014, grew another 71%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.400
in 2015 and rose 31% in 2016. “
From its very first year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.240
DSC generated over $3.5 million in revenue.
A year later, revenue had grown more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.160
sixfold to $19 million.
Then $65 million.&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:20.640
During this time, another competitor,&nbsp;
Harry’s, jumped on the bandwagon too.&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.400
DSC passed $120 million in 2015, then&nbsp;
roughly $240 million just a year later.&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.040
That same year, Michael Dubin sold&nbsp;
Dollar Shave Club for $1 billion.&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.200
At the time, the company had 3.2 million members&nbsp;
and generated $225 million in annual sales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.240
and had raised $160 million in VC funding.
It wasn’t just exploding though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.440
it was draining sales from retail.
“For the 52 weeks ended May 14 [2017],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:53.440
traditional retail sales of razor blades&nbsp;
(including women’s) fell 10.5% to $2.13 billion”.&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:06:01.200
By 2017, Dollar Shave Club had overtaken Gillette&nbsp;
in online market share. 47% to Gillette’s 23%.&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.240
It was far beyond anything Dubin had imagined.
DSC allowed you to skip retailers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:12.480
who always took a cut, and get a lower cost.&nbsp;
And somehow, better products at the same time.&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.080
It really was a fantastic value proposition.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:22.560
So, how did things go from great, to AI ads?
Well… for Dollar Shave Club,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.480
things were going to get much, much worse.
Dubin was still the CEO, but now had to bend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:37.440
to the will of his new overlord: Unilever.
CHAPTER 2 - Cost of Conglomerates&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.720
You might know that name, Unilever. They&nbsp;
own 400 brands, and are one of the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:49.280
consumer products conglomerates, like Nestlé.
Unilever had big plans for Dollar Shave Club.&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.760
But first, they had a problem to solve.
Even though DSC was generating hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.920
of millions of dollars in sales,&nbsp;
it wasn't really making money.&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.480
Even if they had a viral ad… it worked&nbsp;
for about a year. After that, they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:08.400
to compete on regular marketing channels: paid&nbsp;
Facebook, YouTube pre-roll, podcast ads, TV spots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:15.440
and even a Super Bowl ad. Hence, how they ate&nbsp;
into so much of that $160 million VC money.&nbsp;

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:20.080
Soon, competitors like Harry's and even&nbsp;
Gillette copied the subscription model.&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:26.400
But there was another problem. The business model.
DSC was very generous with what you got.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.760
How is that a problem?
Well, most people don’t go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:36.800
through 4 razors in a month. Some might, but many&nbsp;
would go through three, or two, some even one.&nbsp;

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:43.280
So customers cancelled after stockpiling&nbsp;
blades. But, that wasn’t even the biggest issue.&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.800
DSC was competing with conglomerates on price.
Gillette's grip on the market was slipping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:57.520
with its U.S. market share falling from over 70%&nbsp;
in 2010 to 54% by 2016. But wait, isn’t that good?&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.400
Well, the consequences weren’t.
Gillette slashed its prices and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:08.960
admitted it on its own website: "You told us our&nbsp;
blades can be too expensive, and we listened."&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.360
Good for consumers, that’s for sure.
But overnight, Dollar Shave Club's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.480
biggest selling point was gone. All they&nbsp;
had left was their cool, unique brand.&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.520
So, Unilever needed to pivot. They couldn’t&nbsp;
raise the price, but the core products weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.880
doing enough to break even. And that’s&nbsp;
despite a huge defection from Gillette.&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:34.320
In fact, the damage was so severe that P&amp;G&nbsp;
erased $8 billion from Gillette's value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:39.920
years after paying $57 billion to acquire it.
Unilever began launching toothpaste, cologne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:44.800
deodorant, and more under the DSC name.
But in the process, it stopped feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:49.280
like a fun, relatable startup and&nbsp;
started looking more… corporate.&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:54.400
They also moved into Walmart in 2020,&nbsp;
which was definitely bizarre to see.&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:00.080
The economics of DSC just didn’t work.
Sell razors below break even? Great for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:06.160
customers and growth, not for long-term&nbsp;
profit. Profit Unilever wanted.&nbsp;

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:11.120
In January 2021, Dubin sold the last&nbsp;
of his stake, and stepped down as CEO.&nbsp;

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:17.120
By then, Dollar Shave Club was already past its&nbsp;
peak, and I think he saw the writing on the wall.&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:23.120
Subscriber growth had stalled, going from&nbsp;
4M to 3M subs. Revenue had flattened. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.560
company that had once disrupted an entire&nbsp;
industry was beginning to lose momentum.&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.280
Even Unilever admitted it. "We have been clear&nbsp;
that Dollar Shave Club has not delivered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:38.480
results we were expecting. The economics of the&nbsp;
direct-to-consumer channel changed, and we found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:44.240
it harder to unlock non-razor sales than planned,”
Dubin himself no longer seemed convinced by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:46.960
model he had helped popularize.
He later said,&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:51.280
“But I think brands starting out today&nbsp;
would be wise to think: How do they get on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:57.520
omnichannel distribution as fast as they can?”
Omnichannel, meaning online, and in-store.&nbsp;

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:02.560
He realized that this model wasn’t sustainable.&nbsp;
Granted, he had launched a huge brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:08.720
and got a good payout, so good for him!
But, what can you do when the core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:15.840
business model stops working?
CHAPTER 3: Managed Absurdity.&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.960
If you’ve watched this far, consider subscribing!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.960
It helps the channel and means&nbsp;
you won’t miss future videos.

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:28.080
In one final attempt to fix the business,&nbsp;
Unilever borrowed a page from Gillette's playbook.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:33.360
They started offering DSC subscribers free&nbsp;
handles and packs of blades to try out new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.880
cheaper-feeling cartridges.
This was after they had totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:42.480
moved into retail stores, so DSC wasn’t&nbsp;
much different to any other razer brand.&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.480
Then they pulled the Dorco-made&nbsp;
Executive cartridges, and pushed all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:51.120
members onto the new “Club Series razors”.
Customers were unsure where the new razors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:56.720
were from… but they weren’t as good.
"They are floppy and thin. I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:01.680
cancelled my membership after 8 years… it&nbsp;
seems like they are trying to cut costs";&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.200
"They cut me up every time I shave!";
"Canceled my 6-year-old subscription today."&nbsp;

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.720
“New blades are garbage. Going to&nbsp;
see if I can find the Dorco blades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:15.360
on Amazon that everyone seems to like.”
“I also ordered the Dorco Pace 6 blades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:21.360
that are exactly the same as the Executive DSC&nbsp;
blades, but at a lower price. It was a good run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:27.360
DSC. All good things come to an end.”
By this point, Unilever was done.&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.687
They pulled the plug, and bailed out by&nbsp;
selling off 65% to Nexus Capital Management.&nbsp;

00:11:32.687 --> 00:11:34.400
[Hein Schumacher, Unilever CEO]:&nbsp;
"We have made some unsuccessful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:39.040
attempts to move away from our core."
Though Nexus partner Michael Cohen had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:43.600
a different view: "We see growth potential&nbsp;
and will invest in cutting-edge marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.480
product quality, and new innovations… Dollar&nbsp;
Shave Club will also serve as a platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.120
for additional brands with a similar DNA."
[Larry Bodner, New Dollar Shave Club CEO]:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:56.080
"Post-acquisition, Unilever moved the brand&nbsp;
away from its core values while also scaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.120
back its investment in product quality. They&nbsp;
neutered the voice of the brand. They tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.280
to make it too corporate, and they lost&nbsp;
that irreverent, ‘on the edge’ humor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:11.120
And when you do that, you lose the consumer."
“This is where the brand differentiates itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:14.640
in managed absurdity.”
He was right. DSC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:18.800
certainly lost a lot of its charm.
Though, Bodner may have misinterpreted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.320
this “managed absurdity”.
They started cutting costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.680
reducing technology spending by 40%, then&nbsp;
migrating from their own website to Shopify.&nbsp;

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:33.280
Then they moved from LA to North Carolina,&nbsp;
in February 2025, to cut even more costs.&nbsp;

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:36.400
They introduced a Walmart-exclusive&nbsp;
College Razor line.&nbsp;

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:40.000
To bring the brand’s humor back, they&nbsp;
recruited 23 regular customers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:46.800
co-create a new ad campaign and spend a weekend&nbsp;
at its “Order of the Blade” creative workshop.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:22.480
This video has about 600 views, and the feel&nbsp;
here is totally different to the first ad.&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:28.240
The original ads by Dubin felt more grassroots&nbsp;
but were laser focused on the problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:31.360
Razors are annoying, and expensive to buy.&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.520
This was more corporate&nbsp;
pretending to be a “cool brand”.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:46.960
And it gets worse.
I’m unsure if these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:53.440
AI ads came because of this creative workshop or&nbsp;
not… but... yeah these didn't go over too well.&nbsp;

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:57.200
Fans didn’t see it as “managed&nbsp;
absurdity” that Bodner talked about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:02.320
or “putting money where it mattered”.
But for what it was: Cost saving.&nbsp;

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:08.000
Especially when the razors were now worse.
For many, that was the final nail in the coffin.&nbsp;

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:12.880
The products were worse, the brand had lost&nbsp;
its identity, it wasn’t even really a direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:14.400
to consumer product anymore.
GRAND PAYOFF.&nbsp;

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:20.720
As of 2025, DSC's online revenue is down&nbsp;
to just $43 million, granted that doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:24.160
include retail store revenue.
But they’re only forecasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:28.560
to see 0–5% growth in 2026.
You can still buy Dollar Shave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:34.000
Club products today, but the company that sells&nbsp;
them isn't the one people fell in love with.&nbsp;

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:40.560
DSC was a brilliant idea. But unfortunately,&nbsp;
its business model was broken to begin with.&nbsp;

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:46.240
There are two ways to compete: The first is&nbsp;
differentiation, aka being better or different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:50.960
The second is price, being cheaper.
There’s a reason it’s not recommended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:55.280
to compete on price.
But, DSC did both of these.&nbsp;

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:58.960
So they didn’t really have a&nbsp;
good path to profitability.&nbsp;

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:03.280
Hence, all the weird moves to&nbsp;
“fix” a problem that was built in.&nbsp;

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:07.840
There are quite a few brands who dominated&nbsp;
years ago, and are now struggling.&nbsp;

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:13.760
Take Grubhub. They had over 70% of the&nbsp;
delivery market, but were sold off and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:17.200
are now barely surviving.
How do you die after being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:24.720
first and having the entire market?
Click this video to find out how.

